    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p class="lead">If you are thinking about freelancing, or hiring a freelancer, it helps to understand how freelance marketplaces work before you sign up. These platforms decide how you find clients, how much you keep after fees and when you get paid. This guide explains the whole process in plain English, from the first proposal to the money landing in your bank account.</p>

<div class="key-takeaways"><p class="kt-title">Key takeaways</p><ul>
<li>A freelance marketplace matches clients with independent professionals and protects payments, in exchange for a fee.</li>
<li>Platforms typically keep around 10% to 20% of freelancer earnings, so build fees into your rate.</li>
<li>Most marketplaces use escrow: clients fund work up front, and money is released when work is approved.</li>
<li>Freelancers on marketplaces are independent contractors responsible for their own taxes.</li>
</ul></div>

<h2 id="what-is-a-freelance-marketplace">What is a freelance marketplace and how does it work?</h2>
<p>A freelance marketplace is an online platform that connects businesses with independent professionals for short-term or project-based work. Clients post jobs or browse service listings, freelancers send proposals or sell packaged services, and the platform handles contracts, payment protection and reviews in exchange for a service fee taken from each payment.</p>
<p>Think of it as a combination of a job board, a payment processor and a referee. The marketplace does not employ the freelancers. They remain independent businesses, which is why the platform focuses on matching, trust and payments rather than supervising the work. You can read more about the model in Wikipedia's overview of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freelance_marketplace" target="_blank" rel="noopener">freelance marketplace</a>.</p>

<h2 id="how-freelance-marketplaces-work">How freelance marketplaces work, step by step</h2>
<p>Platforms differ in the details, but most follow the same basic flow.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Both sides create profiles.</strong> Freelancers list skills, rates, portfolios and work history. Clients add company details and a payment method. Some platforms verify identity or test skills before approval.</li>
<li><strong>Work gets posted or listed.</strong> Clients post a project with a description, budget and timeline, or freelancers publish fixed-price service packages that clients can buy instantly.</li>
<li><strong>Matching happens.</strong> Freelancers submit proposals, clients invite people from search results, or the platform's algorithm recommends matches based on skills, ratings and history.</li>
<li><strong>A contract is set up.</strong> The two sides agree on scope, price and deadlines inside the platform. This becomes the reference point if a dispute comes up later.</li>
<li><strong>The client funds the work.</strong> For fixed-price jobs, money for the project or first milestone goes into escrow. For hourly jobs, the client is billed for tracked time on a regular cycle.</li>
<li><strong>The freelancer delivers.</strong> Files, messages and revisions are usually handled through the platform so there is a record.</li>
<li><strong>Payment is released.</strong> When the client approves the work, or an auto-approval window passes, funds move to the freelancer's balance, minus the platform fee.</li>
<li><strong>Reviews are left.</strong> Both sides rate each other. These ratings drive future search rankings, which is why reviews matter so much on marketplaces.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Keep all communication and payments on the platform until a contract ends. Taking a client off-platform early can break the terms of service and leaves you without payment protection.</p></div>

<h2 id="types-of-freelance-marketplaces">Types of freelance marketplaces</h2>
<p>Not every platform works the same way. Knowing the main types of freelance marketplaces helps you choose where to spend your time.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>How it works</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Generalist bidding marketplaces</td><td>Clients post projects in almost any category; freelancers submit proposals</td><td>Writers, designers, developers, virtual assistants and most service skills</td></tr>
<tr><td>Service catalog (gig) marketplaces</td><td>Freelancers publish fixed-price packages; clients buy them like products</td><td>Well-defined deliverables such as logos, edits, voiceovers or setup tasks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Niche or specialist marketplaces</td><td>Focus on one field, such as design, development, writing or finance</td><td>Experienced professionals who want clients that value their specialty</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vetted talent networks</td><td>Screen freelancers through tests and interviews, then match them to clients</td><td>Senior specialists who prefer fewer, larger engagements</td></tr>
<tr><td>Local task marketplaces</td><td>Match people for in-person jobs like moving, assembly or repairs</td><td>Handy people and trades with tools and transportation</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Many freelancers start on a generalist platform, then add a niche site once they have reviews and a clear specialty.</p>

<h2 id="freelance-marketplace-fees">Freelance marketplace fees explained</h2>
<p>Freelance marketplace fees are how platforms make money, and they have a direct impact on your income. Fees come in a few forms:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Freelancer service fees.</strong> A percentage of each payment, commonly in the 10% to 20% range. Fiverr, for example, keeps 20% of each order. Some platforms use variable or tiered fees that change based on the contract or client relationship.</li>
<li><strong>Client service or marketplace fees.</strong> A percentage or flat charge added to the client's bill.</li>
<li><strong>Connects, bids or credits.</strong> Some platforms charge small amounts to submit proposals.</li>
<li><strong>Membership plans.</strong> Optional subscriptions for extra proposals, profile visibility or lower fees.</li>
<li><strong>Withdrawal fees.</strong> Charges for some payout methods, currency conversion or instant transfers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Fee structures change often, so always check the platform's current pricing page before you set your rate.</p>

<h3>What fees do to your rate</h3>
<p>Here is how a fee changes what you actually take home on a $1,000 project, before taxes:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Platform fee</th><th>Fee amount</th><th>You receive</th><th>Rate needed to net $1,000</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>5%</td><td>$50</td><td>$950</td><td>$1,053</td></tr>
<tr><td>10%</td><td>$100</td><td>$900</td><td>$1,111</td></tr>
<tr><td>15%</td><td>$150</td><td>$850</td><td>$1,176</td></tr>
<tr><td>20%</td><td>$200</td><td>$800</td><td>$1,250</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The formula is simple: divide what you want to net by (1 minus the fee). To net $1,000 on a 20% platform, you need to charge $1,000 / 0.80 = $1,250.</p>

