<h2 id="how-freelancers-get-paid">How freelancers get paid</h2>
<p>Payment protection is the main reason many freelancers put up with marketplace fees. Here is how freelancers get paid on most platforms.</p>
<h3>Fixed-price and milestone contracts</h3>
<p>The client deposits money for the project, or for each milestone, before work begins. The platform holds it in escrow. When you submit the work, the client approves it or requests revisions. If the client goes silent, many platforms release payment automatically after a set number of days.</p>
<h3>Hourly contracts</h3>
<p>You log time with the platform's tracker, which may capture screenshots or activity levels as proof of work. The client is billed weekly or on a regular cycle. Hourly protection usually only covers time tracked through the platform's own tool.</p>
<h3>Clearance periods and withdrawals</h3>
<p>Once released, funds often sit in a pending or clearance period, commonly several days to about two weeks, so the platform can handle refunds and fraud checks. After that you can withdraw to a bank account, PayPal, a wire transfer or other supported methods. Plan your cash flow around this lag, especially when you are new.</p>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Watch out for scams:</strong> Be wary of anyone who asks you to pay a fee to get a job, cash a check and send money back, or move to an outside messaging app before a contract is in place. Legitimate clients pay through the platform.</p></div>

<h2 id="how-clients-hire">How clients hire on freelance marketplaces</h2>
<p>Understanding the client side helps freelancers win more work. Most clients follow a short process: they write a project brief, set a budget, review proposals or search profiles, shortlist a few candidates, run a quick interview or paid test, then hire.</p>
<p>Clients usually judge freelancers on:</p>
<ul>
<li>a proposal that speaks directly to their project, not a template,</li>
<li>relevant portfolio samples,</li>
<li>ratings, reviews and job success history,</li>
<li>responsiveness in the first few messages, and</li>
<li>a clear price and timeline.</li>
</ul>
<p>A polished profile does a lot of this work for you. Our guide to <a href="/building-a-professional-freelancer-profile/">building a professional freelancer profile</a> covers headlines, portfolios and pricing in detail.</p>

<h2 id="trust-and-disputes">Trust, safety and dispute resolution</h2>
<p>Marketplaces earn their fees by reducing risk on both sides. Common safeguards include identity verification, two-way reviews, escrow, time tracking for hourly work and in-platform messaging records. If a project goes wrong, most platforms offer a dispute process: first the two parties try to resolve it, then a platform mediator reviews the contract, messages and deliverables. Some platforms offer formal arbitration for larger disputes, sometimes for a fee.</p>
<p>The best protection is a clear scope at the start. Write down deliverables, number of revisions and deadlines in the contract, not just in chat.</p>

<h2 id="taxes-for-marketplace-freelancers">Taxes for marketplace freelancers</h2>
<p>When you work through a marketplace you are almost always an independent contractor, not an employee of the platform or the client. The IRS explains the distinction on its page defining an <a href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/independent-contractor-defined" target="_blank" rel="noopener">independent contractor</a>. That means no taxes are withheld from your earnings.</p>
<p>Platforms that process payments may send you a Form 1099-K if you pass $20,000 and 200 transactions in a year. Payments made directly by clients in 2026 trigger a 1099-NEC only at $2,000 or more. Below those thresholds you may get no form at all, but the income is still taxable. You will owe 15.3% self-employment tax on most of your net profit plus income tax, and platform fees are a deductible business expense. This is general information, not tax advice; our guide to <a href="/independent-contractor-tax-responsibilities/">independent contractor taxes</a> walks through quarterly payments and deductions.</p>

<h2 id="pros-and-cons">Advantages and limitations</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Limitations</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Steady stream of clients without cold outreach</td><td>Fees reduce your effective rate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payment protection through escrow</td><td>Heavy competition, especially for beginners</td></tr>
<tr><td>Built-in contracts, invoicing and messaging</td><td>Price pressure from low bids</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reviews build credibility fast</td><td>Algorithms and policy changes affect visibility</td></tr>
<tr><td>Access to clients worldwide</td><td>Limits on taking clients off-platform</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2 id="choosing-a-marketplace">How to choose the right freelance marketplace</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Match the platform to your work.</strong> Packaged deliverables fit catalog sites; custom projects fit bidding sites; senior expertise fits vetted networks.</li>
<li><strong>Compare total fees.</strong> Add service fees, proposal costs and withdrawal charges to see your real take.</li>
<li><strong>Check demand.</strong> Search your skill and count recent projects and their budgets.</li>
<li><strong>Read the payment terms.</strong> Look at escrow rules, clearance periods and payout options.</li>
<li><strong>Start with one or two.</strong> Focus your effort until you have a few strong reviews, then expand.</li>
</ol>
<p>Freelancing is not the only way to get flexible work. If you would rather have steady paychecks with taxes withheld, temp and contract roles through Best Jobs Pro offer short-term flexibility as a W-2 employee. Our recruiters often suggest pairing a part-time assignment with freelance projects while you build a client base. Browse current <a href="/job-postings/">job postings</a> to see what is available.</p>

